Oh, I didn't know you had a man down here with you.

JUDD  
Nan's we're really b-

EDITH  
-Judd, go fix the fuckin' wifi so your nan can get back to her ... studies.

Judd exits the basement.

EDITH (CONT’D)
Whats your name big boy?

FRANKO  
Uhh Frank.

EDITH  
What a strong name. So Frank - have you ever been with a woman who knows exactly what she's doin' ... down there?

FRANKO  
Uhhh what do you mean?

EDITH
I swallow loads like a washing machine on heavy duty.

FRANKO  
(thinking fast)
I'm actually gay.

EDITH  
So was my ex-husband.

FRANKO  
I have kids.

EDITH  
Judd can watch em.

FRANKO  
I have herpes.

EDITH  
Makes two of us.

Franko's run out of ideas. Judd enters the basement.

JUDD  
Wifi's back up.

EDITH  
Judd. You didn't tell me you had a homosexual friend.  
(to Judd)
Somethin' you wanna tell me?

JUDD  
Huh?

EDITH  
Ya know. Are ya - a little light in the loafers?

JUDD  
What?

EDITH  
Are ya playing for the other team dear?

JUDD  
Nans what are you ...

Franko signals to Judd to go along with it.

JUDD  
Yup.

EDITH  
Honey.  
(hugs Judd)
You can always tell me anything alright. Especially something like this.

JUDD  
Ok.

Edith walks towards the exit.

EDITH  
Well I guess I'll leave you two love birds alone then. Ta Ta!

Edith exits.

FRANKO  
Dude, the FBI’s literally tracking their ass-gadget to this address right now. We need to get our twenty grand and dip, or we’re fucked six ways to Sunday.

## Beat 14: Edith Unknowingly Blocks FBI While Criminals Escape
EXT. JUDD'S HOUSE - DAY #24#

Franko and Judd pop out the back of the house just as Randall and Jensen pull up to the front.

RANDALL  
This is it. Last known coordinates.

Randall walks up to the front door and knocks.

EDITH (O.S.)  
I told you last time! The only way I'm gonna become a Mormon is if you fuck my fuckin' brains ou—

The door swings open to reveal EDITH, wearing a housecoat that's seen better decades.

RANDALL  
Agent Randall, FBI. 

Edith wants to fuck Agent Randall.

RANDALL (CONT’D)
I believe a piece of government property is currently on your premises.

EDITH
(seductive)
Really? What’s it look like?

RANDALL
I'm looking for something about-
(hand gestures)
-this long and this thick. Electronic in nature.

EDITH
A lot of things fit that description, honey. You'll have to be more... specific.

RANDALL
The device may be emitting a buzzing sound.

EDITH
They all do that.

RANDALL
How about I just take a look around?

EDITH
Let me get this straight. You are an FBI agent who wants to look around my house. Now why in the fuck would I do that?

RANDALL
(deadpan)
It’s a matter of national security.

EDITH
You got a warrant, Agent Whatever.

RANDALL
Agent Randall.

EDITH
That your first name or your last name?

RANDALL
Agent Randall Cunningham.

EDITH
Sounds fake. 
(a beat)
No warrant, no basement. That's how America works. I watch Fox News. I know my rights.

RANDALL
Look, Mrs...?

EDITH
Miss. Five times.

RANDALL
Miss Edith. I'm not here to disrupt your day or... whatever activities you were anticipating when you thought I was a Mormon missionary.

EDITH
You'd be surprised what those boys are into once they're off the bike.

RANDALL
My point is, I can be in and out in five minutes—

EDITH
(interrupting)
-that's what my fourth husband said. He wore my pantyhose and ate lean cuisines.

Randall blinks with the careful neutrality of a man who's just been handed information he absolutely cannot process right now.

RANDALL
Five minutes in your basement, or I come back with a warrant and I take you down to the station for-
(a beat)
-obstruction of justice.

Edith looks at him for a beat.

EDITH
What’s this rectal probe look like again?

RANDALL
(shows picture)
It's 5 inch long cylinder. Black. Government property, so it has a small eagle embossed on it.

EDITH
The government puts its logo on things it shoves up people's asses?

RANDALL
The government puts its logo on everything it owns.

EDITH
If I was putting things inside someone's colon, I wouldn't stamp it with my return address.

Randall stares at her with new appreciation, like he's just discovered a gas station sandwich that doesn't cause immediate regret.

RANDALL
You actually make a fair point.

She takes one last, contemplative drag of her cigarette before flicking it into a potted plant that appears to have died during the Clinton administration.

EDITH
Fine. Five minutes in the basement.

She guides Randall through the house, to the entrance to the basement.

EDITH
The boys were down there earlier. I think I walked in on them having gay sex or something. 

RANDALL
The boys?

EDITH
My nephew Judd and his boyfriend. They come and go. Mostly they use the basement for what they call "projects."

She leads Randall down a hallway cluttered with framed photos of cats wearing holiday-themed costumes. None of the cats look happy about their career in modeling.

EDITH
Basement's through here. Watch your step.

She swings open the door to reveal stairs descending into darkness. A smell wafts up that suggests chemistry has recently been performed by someone whose understanding of the subject came primarily from breaking bad and YouTube tutorials with suspicious view counts.

RANDALL
What is that smell?

EDITH
Boys will be boys officer.

Randall begins his descent, hand instinctively moving to his sidearm. Each step creaks like it's auditioning for a horror movie sound effect.

INT. JUDD'S HOUSE - BASEMENT - CONTINUOUS #25#

Randall reaches the bottom. Stops.

The blue kiddie pool. Chemical stains bleeding across the concrete like a Rorschach test. The air still burns. Yellowish residue coats the plastic rim.

Jensen hangs back on the stairs, one hand covering his nose.

Randall crouches beside the pool. Studies the stain pattern. The empty chemical bottles lined up like soldiers. The tarp bunched in the corner.

He pulls out his phone. Starts taking photos. Methodical. Every angle. The pool. The bottles. The chemical burns on the floor. Close-up of the drain where something thick and wrong has settled.

RANDALL
(quiet, to Jensen)
They dissolved him.

JENSEN
In a kiddie pool?

Randall photographs the manufacturer labels on the chemical bottles. The mason jar. The pool skimmer propped against the wall, still discolored.

RANDALL
Get forensics here. Quietly. No local PD.

He stands. Takes one more photo — wide shot, the whole scene. A blue plastic kiddie pool in a grandmother's basement, surrounded by the chemistry of disappearance.

From upstairs, Edith's voice carries down with the penetrating quality of someone who's spent decades being heard over bar noise.

EDITH
You find your ass-bug yet? Your five minutes is up, and I've got a Mormon coming back to explain the Book of Something-or-Other.

Randall pockets his phone. Looks at Jensen.

RANDALL
Whoever did this is not Chinese.

The CRACK of a pool ball—

CUT TO:

## Beat 15: Dominic Pays Only $500 of Promised $20k
INT. LOU'S RESTAURANT #26#

DOMINIC and BOBBY circle the pool table like sharks with cue sticks. The crack of balls echoes off the empty restaurant.

The door opens. FRANKO and JUDD enter, looking like they're walking into their own funeral.

DOMINIC
Boys!

FRANKO
Sup.

JUDD
We’d like to get our money and go now.

Dominic lines up a shot, sinks the eight ball with unnecessary flair.

DOMINIC
Business men. I like that.

He reaches into his jacket, pulls out a roll of bills. Slaps it on the felt.

DOMINIC (CONT'D)
Five hundred.

Franko and Judd exchange a look that could curdle milk.

FRANKO
The fuck is this? We melted a whole-ass human being for you.

JUDD
(to bobby)
You agreed to twenty thousand dude!

DOMINIC
(shrugging)
Must've been a miscommunication. But hey—

They all start shifting around the table now, a slow dance of positioning.

DOMINIC (CONT'D)
—there's plenty more where that came from. You boys stay available, stay useful, and—
(a beat)
-skies the limit.

Franko and Judd are like “really bro”?

JUDD
(to Dominic)
Actually you know what. We’re good.

Judd motions Franko.

JUDD (CONT’D)
How about we take that little FBI device we found in queef-for-brains after we liquefied him back to its owner?

FRANKO
Yeah. I bet they wanna know all about how he was shitting right before you capped him.

The temperature drops ten degrees. Bobby's hand drifts toward his waistband.

DOMINIC
Excuse me?

FRANKO
Yeah, bro. Dude had a fuckin' FBI AirTag shoved up his prison wallet. High-tech anal surveillance or some shit.

Dominic's face cycles through confusion, rage, and calculation.

DOMINIC
So like, you're just gonna walk into the FBI and be all. 
(valley girl voice)
"Hey guys, it's Judd! Yeah, ummm, I just melted a guy and found your super advanced butt plug and I wanted to, like, return it?"

JUDD
Nah. Was thinking more like raid his stash and sell his shit on eBay.

DOMINIC
He didn't have shit in that cottage.

FRANKO
Wrong again, chief. We plugged the ass-gadget into a computer. Besides giving us the complete history of his bowel movements, it showed us this storage locker he was hitting every other day.

Dominic sets down his cue stick. The game has changed.

DOMINIC 
What do you want?

JUDD
The 20K for starters.

DOMINIC
Bobbys an idiot. There’s no way I’m paying you 20K.

FRANKO
10K plus we get to sell shit from bros storage locker.

DOMINIC
Alright. But I’m going with you. I want to see ... how did you put it ... “queef-for-brains” was doing every other day at this place.

JUDD
Aight.

FRANKO
Just follow us. And bring the money this time, not some bullshit IOU.

They head for the door, leaving Lou's as empty as they found it.

## Beat 16: Randall Reveals Partner’s Murder Drives Him
INT. FBI OFFICE #27#

Evidence photos plaster a board: Sal’s bloody cottage, Judd’s basement, the acid-filled kiddie pool. Randall obsessively arranges surveillance photos. Jensen watches him work.

JENSEN
(tentative)
Can I ask you something?

RANDALL
If it’s about the requisition forms for the lab work, I already—

JENSEN
It’s not that. I’m just curious... how does someone end up specializing in... you know... this particular type of surveillance?

Randall’s focus remains on the board.

RANDALL
You mean why am I shoving government hardware up informants’ asses instead of climbing the Bureau ladder like a good little agent?

JENSEN
When most people talk about surveillance, they think wiretaps.

Something in Randall’s posture softens almost imperceptibly.

RANDALL
Three years ago, I had a partner. Marcus. Best agent I ever worked with. He went deep cover in Santoro’s organization.

Jensen leans against a desk, sensing this isn’t a story Randall tells often.

RANDALL (CONT’D)
Two months in, comms stop. A week later, they find parts of him in seven different dumpsters across the city.

JENSEN
Jesus.

RANDALL
The Medical Examiner said he’d been alive for most of it.

Jensen winces.

RANDALL (CONT’D)
Bureau wanted to close the case. Not enough evidence to connect it directly to Santoro.
(a beat)
Can’t get rid of evidence if the evidence is inside you, sending real-time updates to a secure server.

Randall turns to the centerpiece of the evidence board: a blurry surveillance photo of DOMINIC, face partially obscured.

RANDALL (CONT’D)
Dominic Santoro. Ghost in the machine. Three years, forty-seven informants, over two hundred counts of associated crimes, and I’ve never even seen his face in person.

JENSEN
Forty-seven?

Randall doesn’t explain. Pins another photo to the board.

JENSEN (CONT’D)
(studying the photo)
Not much to look at anyway.

RANDALL
(with intensity)
It’s not about his face. It’s about looking him in the eyes.

A beat.

RANDALL (CONT’D)
I want him to know it was me.

Jensen looks at Randall with new understanding — and a touch of concern.

JENSEN
You think Sal was close? To getting real evidence on Santoro?

RANDALL
Sal was closer than anyone.
(a beat)
Which means his storage unit is the most important room in this investigation. Everything he gathered on Santoro is in there.

Randall pulls a set of coordinates from the board and hands them to Jensen.

RANDALL (CONT’D)
I need you to get over there tonight. Catalog everything. Prep it for extraction before someone else figures out what Sal had.

JENSEN
(taking the coordinates)
You’re not coming?

RANDALL
I’ve got the Deputy Director breathing down my neck about the dead informant. Damage control.
(a beat)
It’s just a storage unit, Jensen. In and out.

Jensen nods. Heads for the door.

RANDALL (CONT’D)
Jensen.

Jensen turns.

Randall looks at him. A beat. Whatever he was going to say, he swallows it.

RANDALL (CONT’D)
Take pictures of everything before you move it.

JENSEN
Yes sir.

Jensen exits.

## Beat 17: Criminals Break Into Sal’s Evidence Storage
EXT. STORAGE FACILITY - NIGHT #28#

Franko’s beat-up car pulls into the poorly lit parking lot. Dominic’s sedan follows.

Franko and Judd exit, looking around nervously. Dominic and Bobby emerge from their car, unhurried.

JUDD
You sure this is the place?

