—locations that, quite frankly, we cannot specify during family viewing hours—

## Beat 32: Congressional Hearings Expose Anal Surveillance

INT. CONGRESSIONAL HEARING - DAY #95#

A panel of stone-faced SENATORS sits in judgment of a sweating INTELLIGENCE DIRECTOR who looks like he'd rather be waterboarded than continue this conversation.

INTELLIGENCE DIRECTOR
The program, codenamed "Operation Inside Man," was developed as a means of gathering intelligence on criminal organizations through... unconventional methods.

SENATOR #1
(leaning into microphone)
"Unconventional methods" meaning you put government property up American citizens' rectums without their knowledge or consent?

INTELLIGENCE DIRECTOR
(adjusting tie)
That characterization lacks certain... nuances.

SENATOR #2
(removing glasses dramatically)
Sir, there is nothing nuanced about non-consensual anal surveillance.

The gallery erupts in a mixture of outrage and uncomfortable laughter.

EXT. PROTEST - DAY #96#

Outside a federal building, PROTESTERS wave hand-painted signs: "THE GOVERNMENT IS UP MY ASS (LITERALLY)," "HANDS OFF MY HINEY," and "MY COLON, MY CHOICE."

A PROTEST LEADER with a megaphone leads a chant that's equal parts righteous indignation and middle-school humor.

PROTEST LEADER
WHAT DO WE WANT?

CROWD
PRIVACY FOR OUR PRIVATES!

PROTEST LEADER
WHEN DO WE WANT IT?

CROWD
AFTER A THOROUGH CAVITY SEARCH!

## Beat 33: Dominic Monetizes Trauma as Self-Help Guru

INT. DOMINICS NEW HOUSE - DAY #97#

CLOSE ON: A TV SCREEN

On screen, DOMINIC stands in a warmly lit studio space, wearing a tasteful sweater over a collared shirt. Soft piano music plays. The production value screams “late-night infomercial meets therapy session.”

ON-SCREEN DOMINIC
(earnest, therapeutic)
Welcome back to Module Seven of “Measuring Up: A Journey Beyond the Physical.” I’m Dominic Santoro, and today we’re talking about letting go.

He walks slowly toward the camera, hands clasped.

ON-SCREEN DOMINIC (CONT’D)
For years, I held onto things that didn’t serve me. Physical reminders of my inadequacy. Trophies that only reinforced my deepest insecurities.
(beat, meaningful look)
Maybe you’re holding onto something too. A collection. A grudge. A part of someone that was never really yours to keep.

PULL BACK to reveal:

The real DOMINIC sits on a white leather couch, watching intently. Beside him, MARCUS (30s, hipster glasses, MacBook balanced on knee) takes notes.

ON-SCREEN DOMINIC (CONT’D)
Remember our mantra: Focus on people. Not their parts.

The video Dominic places his hand over his heart.

ON-SCREEN DOMINIC (CONT’D)
Say it with me now.

The real Dominic mouths along unconsciously.

ON-SCREEN DOMINIC (CONT’D)
Focus on people. Not their parts. Focus on the whole person. The soul behind the... anatomy.

The video transitions to a graphic: “LETTING GO EXERCISE” with clip art of hands releasing butterflies.

Real Dominic hits pause.

DOMINIC
This is perfect. My only notes are... can we make the butterflies more masculine? Maybe eagles? Or bullets transforming into doves?

MARCUS
(typing)
Bullets to doves. Got it.

DOMINIC
And when I say “anatomy,” maybe we punch in tighter on my face? Really let them see the pain I’m transcending?

MARCUS
We could add a tear.

DOMINIC
(considering)
Digital or practical?

MARCUS
I was thinking we film a pickup. More authentic.

DOMINIC
I like where your head’s at.
(standing, pacing)
What’s the presale number on the complete package?

MARCUS
Forty-seven thousand units.

Dominic stops. That number.

DOMINIC
(quiet)
Forty-seven.

MARCUS
Is that... significant?

DOMINIC
(shaking it off)
No. Just synchronicity. The universe speaking.

He moves to the floor-to-ceiling windows. The city sprawls below.

DOMINIC (CONT’D)
You know what’s beautiful, Marcus? I spent years collecting evidence of other men’s superiority. Now I’m teaching men to find superiority within themselves.

MARCUS
That’s module twelve.

DOMINIC
Right. “The Biggest Dick Is Self-Acceptance.”

MARCUS
We’re still workshopping that title.

Dominic’s new assistant CANDY (late 40s, built like a retired linebacker) enters carrying a box of promotional materials. Crop top that stops four inches above his navel. Khakis pulled up to his ribs. Gold chain. Mustache that means business. Nobody comments on any of this. T-shirts reading “I MEASURE UP” and “SIZE DOESN’T MATTER (IT’S WHAT’S INSIDE).”

CANDY
Boss, the merchandise came in.

DOMINIC
(examining a shirt)
Beautiful. Suffering transformed into self-help. Violence into wisdom. 
(to Marcus)
Make sure the documentary crew gets this. The merch arrival is very “hero’s journey.”

MARCUS
They’re already here. They want to film your meditation session.

DOMINIC
Tell them twenty minutes. I need to practice my vulnerable face.

As Marcus exits, Dominic holds up one of the shirts to CANDY.

DOMINIC (CONT’D)
(to himself)
We’ve come a long way from preserving trophies in formaldehyde, haven’t we?

CANDY
Huh?

DOMINIC
Nevermind-
-now we preserve souls. Metaphorically.
(beat)
The profit margins are so much better.

He returns to the TV, rewinding his own image, studying it like a man who’s finally learned to love what he sees.

DOMINIC (CONT’D)
(to his on-screen self)
Focus on people. Not their parts.

The camera pushes in on his face—serene, delusional, and absolutely convinced he’s found his calling.

## Beat 34: Toast for Bobby at Lou's

INT. LOU'S RESTAURANT - DAY #98#

The same booth. The same everything. Franko and Judd sit across from each other, beers in front of them. The place is as empty as it was the first time.

A beat. Just the hum of a bar that hasn't changed since it opened.

FRANKO
You think he knew?

JUDD
Knew what?

FRANKO
That he wasn't gonna make it out of there.

Judd doesn't answer. Peels the label on his beer.

JUDD
He said he'd never felt like he belonged somewhere.

FRANKO
Yeah.

JUDD
Dude belonged.

Franko raises his beer. Judd does the same.

FRANKO
To Bobby.

JUDD
To Bobby.

They clink. Drink. Set the bottles down.

FRANKO
Constipated fuck.

JUDD
The most constipated.

A beat. The weight of it. Then Franko checks his phone.

FRANKO
Nans gets out in twenty.

JUDD
Let's go.

They leave cash on the table and head for the door. The booth sits empty behind them — the same booth where Bobby first showed them a body in a trunk and everything went sideways.

## Beat 35: Edith Released as Judd Gains Whistleblower Status
EXT. COUNTY JAIL - PARKING LOT - DAY #99#

Judd and Franko lean against Franko’s beat-up car in the visitor’s lot. The county jail looms behind them, all concrete and disappointment.

FRANKO  
This is getting ridiculous. This dude the other day asked me to sign his lower back.

JUDD  
What?

FRANKO  
Like, lifted his shirt in the restaurant while I was trying to eat my chicken tenders.

JUDD  
Could be worse. This chick at the gas station yesterday asked if I’d check if she had a tracker.

FRANKO  
What’d you say?

JUDD  
Offered me tree ‘fiddy.

FRANKO  
Nice.

JUDD  
I told her I’d need specialized equipment and a government clearance.

FRANKO  
For reals?

JUDD  
Naw I tapped it.

FRANKO  
Tight.

They share an uncomfortable laugh. Judd checks his phone.

JUDD  
She should be out any minute.

FRANKO  
You think she’s gonna be pissed?

PAN TO:

The jail’s exit gate in the distance. It opens with a BUZZ. A tiny figure emerges, flanked by two GUARDS.

EDITH (O.S.)  
(distant but loud)  
If you aren’t gonna give me a pat down then get your fuckin’ hands off me!

The guards retreat. Edith begins her long walk across the parking lot, still in her ratty housecoat, carrying a clear plastic bag of belongings.

EDITH (CONT’D)  
(getting louder as she approaches)  
FORTY-EIGHT HOURS! You wouldn’t believe - there isn’t a SINGLE man in that place! Even the guards are women - but I’m pretty sure they were all lesbians. A woman cannot live on scissoring alone!

She’s halfway across now, arms gesturing wildly.

EDITH (CONT’D)  
They got me sleeping next to this lady who kept telling me she killed her husband with a Vitamix. I’m just looking at her like bitch I don’t think you even know what a Vitamix is!

Getting closer. Judd and Franko straighten up.

EDITH (CONT’D)  
But I did learn a couple little tax tricks that will definitely come in handy. Did you know that you can-

She stops in front of them, catching her breath. Looks at the car.

EDITH (CONT’D)  
-you guys uncover a government plot and you’re still drivin’ this piece of shit?

Judd gives her a meaningful look.

JUDD  
I’m sorry you got involved in this Nans.

EDITH  
Let’s get one thing straight. I am not involved in any of your shit. But-  
(beat)  
-there is a kind of social currency being The Kiddie Pool Killer’s grandmother.

She turns to Franko.

EDITH (CONT’D)  
You’re the gay one right?

Franko and Judd exchange looks.

FRANKO  
Yes ma’am.

EDITH  
So are you gonna tell me how in the fuck you aren’t in jail yourself?

JUDD  
Cuz we’re whistleblowers Nans. According to the government we’re heroes now.

Edith processes this as she climbs into the back seat. Her face says *the world is going to hell*.

EDITH
That is just the craziest shit I have ever heard.

INT. FRANKO’S CAR - CONTINUOUS #100#

Edith climbs in the back. Immediately rolls down the window. Digs through the car’s ashtray, finds an ancient cigarette, lights it.

EDITH (CONT’D)
So what are you doing now? If you’re smart you’ll turn this into a media empire like all the other famous serial killers did.

JUDD
Actually, we’ve got a book deal lined up. Seven figures Nans.

EDITH
(immediate)
I want my cut. Prison consulting fee.
(takes a drag)
Let’s get the fuck out of here.

Franko starts the car. They pull away, smoke pouring from Edith’s window.

## Beat 36: Randall Pivots Surveillance Tech to Silicon Valley
INT. VENTURE CAPITAL CONFERENCE ROOM - DAY #101#

Floor-to-ceiling windows frame a Silicon Valley vista. A massive screen displays “CONSUMERTRACK™ - THE FUTURE OF INTIMATE ANALYTICS.”

RANDALL stands at the head of a gleaming conference table, laser pointer in hand. He wears an ill-fitting blazer over a startup-branded t-shirt that reads “DISRUPT FROM WITHIN.” 

Around the table: Three VENTURE CAPITALISTS in identical Patagonia vests.

RANDALL
Gentlemen, what if I told you we could know not just what your customers buy, but exactly how they process it? When they process it? The quality of that processing?

He clicks. A slide appears: “THE $847 BILLION DIGESTIVE DATA MARKET.”

VC #1
(leaning forward)
Go on.

RANDALL
Current analytics tell you someone bought your premium yogurt. But did they actually digest it? Did it cause discomfort? Did they have a positive bathroom experience afterward?

Click. New slide: A human silhouette with data points throughout the digestive system.

RANDALL (CONT’D)
ConsumerTrack™ provides real-time gastrointestinal insights. Imagine A/B testing your food products based on actual bowel responses.

VC #2
The implementation seems... invasive.

RANDALL
(practiced smile)
That’s the beauty. We partner with colonoscopy clinics, gastroenterologists, even yoga studios. “Free health screening with our complimentary wellness device.”

VC #3
What’s the hardware cost?

RANDALL
Negligible. I’ve spent three years perfecting the technology under... previous funding.

He clicks to a slide showing the device, but with a sleek Apple-esque redesign.

RANDALL (CONT’D)
We’re not selling surveillance. We’re selling wellness insights. Peace of mind. The quantified self, taken to its logical conclusion.

VC #1
What about privacy concerns?

RANDALL
(without missing a beat)
Users sign a forty-page terms of service. It’s all there in section 23, subsection J. Full anonymized data sharing with our “wellness partners.”

The VCs exchange glances. Greed wrestling with whatever remains of their conscience.

VC #2
The data potential is incredible. Food companies alone would pay millions.

VC #3
Pharmaceutical companies. Insurance providers. 

VC #1
Dating apps. Imagine matching based on compatible gut biomes.

They’re getting excited now, the moral implications evaporating like morning mist in Silicon Valley.

RANDALL
Phase one: Health and wellness sector. Phase two: Corporate wellness programs. “Employers who care about their team’s digestive health.”

VC #2
Phase three?

RANDALL
(clicking to a world map)
Global deployment. Starting with countries that have... flexible privacy laws.

VC #1 stands, extending his hand.

VC #1
Randall, I think you’ve found your Series A.

The others stand. Handshakes all around. 

VC #3
How soon can you have a prototype?

RANDALL
(touching his lower back unconsciously)
I’m beta testing one myself. Three months of continuous operation, zero failures.

VC #2
(popping champagne)
To ConsumerTrack™. Disrupting privacy from the inside out.

They toast. Randall raises his glass, a smile spreading across his face—the smile of a man who’s found his true calling in the worst possible way.

RANDALL
To knowing our customers... intimately.

They drink. Through the windows, Silicon Valley sprawls below—thousands of potential rectums waiting to be monetized.

The camera slowly zooms in on Randall’s face. His eyes gleam with the fervor of a true believer who’s simply changed churches.

FADE TO:

TITLE CARD: “ConsumerTrack™ IPO’d for $2.3 billion in 2027”

TITLE CARD: “Randall’s net worth: $847 million”

TITLE CARD: “Number of active units deployed: [REDACTED]”

THE END